<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-where-do-colors-come-from" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Where do Colors Come From?</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Bleaching is a common process that involves the whitening of fabrics and the removal of stains.
      But to understand the chemistry involved in this mechanism, as well as how dyes work, we need to explore something more fundamental: <idx><h>bleaching</h></idx> <idx><h>dye</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Where do colors actually come from?
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-light-is-a-wave">
    <title>Light is a Wave</title>

    <p>
      For most of our history, we had no idea what light was.
      This was quite a troubling mystery <mdash/> we knew nothing about light, yet our eyes used it all the time to examine the world.
      After centuries of work by physicists, chemists, and biologists, we now know that light is a <term>wave</term> (more specifically a traveling wave), and we have an understanding of how chemicals and our eyes interact with it.
      Let's unpack what that means.
      <idx><h>light</h></idx> <idx><h>wave</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-journey-of-light">
      <caption>The journey of light, from a light source to your eye.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/journey-of-light.png" width="50%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      When we look at a single bit of light (called a <term>photon</term> ), it does three things:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            It is emitted by a light source like a light bulb;
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            It travels across space; and
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            It is absorbed by a light receiver like your eyes.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <idx><h>photon</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      The reason we call it a <q>wave</q> is that it has a few properties and phenomena associated with waves.
      Specifically, it has a <term>wavelength</term>, denoted with the Greek letter <foreign>lambda</foreign> <m>\lambda</m>, which tells us how long the distance between different peaks of the wave is.
      The wavelength is what determines the color of a photon.
      <idx><h>wavelength</h></idx> <idx><h>lambda</h><see>wavelength</see></idx> <idx><h>color</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Wavelengths span a spectrum called the <term>electromagnetic spectrum</term>, and our eyes can only perceive a small part of this spectrum.
      We call this the visible spectrum; see <xref ref="fig-visible-spectrum"/>.
      <idx><h>electromagnetic spectrum</h></idx> <idx><h>visible spectrum</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-visible-spectrum">
      <caption>The visible part of the electromagnetic spectrum.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/visible-spectrum.png" width="80%"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-absorption-of-light-is-related-to-electronic-transitions-between-molecular-orbitals">
    <title>The Absorption of Light is Related to Electronic Transitions Between Molecular Orbitals</title>

    <p>
      As mentioned in the previous section, light must be emitted by a source and then absorbed to be observed.
      How does this actually happen?
    </p>

    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="subsec-thermal-energy-is-transferred-through-conduction-convection-and-radiation"/> that radiation is one way thermal energy can be transferred.
      This is because photons themselves carry energy that is based on their wavelengths.
      The shorter the photon's wavelength, the higher their energy is.
      <idx><h>radiation</h></idx> <idx><h>photon</h><h>energy of</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      When electrons orbit an atom, they occupy spaces called <term>atomic orbitals</term>.
      When atoms combine to form a molecule, however, the atomic orbitals combine to form spaces called <term>molecular orbitals</term>, often abbreviated as MOs.
      <idx><h>atomic orbital</h></idx> <idx><h>molecular orbital</h></idx> <idx><h>MO</h><see>molecular orbital</see></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mo-diagram-hydrogen">
      <caption>An example of the formation of molecular orbitals using hydrogen. The two individual atomic orbitals on the two atoms form two molecular orbitals when they bond together.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/mo-diagram-hydrogen.png" width="60%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Each MO has a specific energy level, and electrons occupy them starting with the lowest energy level.
      They will occupy the MOs until there are no electrons left; the last electron occupies the <term>highest occupied molecular orbital</term> (HOMO).
      The orbital right above it is, as one would expect, the <term>lowest unoccupied molecular orbital</term> (LUMO).
      You can see an example of a diagram with the HOMO and LUMO labeled in <xref ref="fig-mo-diagram-oxygen"/>.
      <idx><h>highest occupied molecular orbital</h></idx> <idx><h>HOMO</h><see>highest occupied molecular orbital</see></idx> <idx><h>lowest unoccupied molecular orbital</h></idx> <idx><h>LUMO</h><see>lowest unoccupied molecular orbital</see></idx> <idx><h>molecular orbital diagram</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mo-diagram-oxygen">
      <caption>An example of a molecular orbital diagram. Each horizontal line represents one molecular orbital, capable of fitting two electrons. A higher placement represents higher energy. Incidentally, this is the MO diagram for oxygen gas, <m>\ce{O2}</m>.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/mo-diagram-oxygen.png" width="50%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Now suppose a photon is traveling towards the molecule.
      It carries a specific energy <mdash/> let's call it <m>E</m>.
      The molecule has electrons that occupy the HOMO.
      If <m>E</m> is <em>exactly</em> enough to boost the electron onto the LUMO, then the electron will absorb the photon.
      We say that it gets <term>excited</term> by the photon ( <xref ref="fig-excitation-of-electrons"/> ).
      <idx><h>electron</h><h>excited</h></idx> <idx><h>absorption (of light)</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-excitation-of-electrons">
      <caption>When a photon reaches an electron, and its energy matches the HOMO-LUMO energy gap, the electron gets excited and absorbs the photon.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/excitation-of-electrons.png" width="80%"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Importantly, the energy of the photon must <em>exactly</em> be the energy of the energy gap between the HOMO and LUMO, no more and no less.
      If the energy does not match, it simply passes through the molecule or gets reflected.
      <idx><h>HOMO-LUMO gap</h></idx> <idx><h>reflection (of light)</h></idx>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-when-one-color-is-absorbed-the-complementary-color-is-observed">
    <title>When One Color is Absorbed, the Complementary Color is Observed</title>

    <p>
      It may seem confusing why white light is not part of the spectrum in <xref ref="fig-visible-spectrum"/>.
      The answer is that white light is actually the combination of all wavelengths of visible light.
      <idx><h>white light</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, suppose white light is shined onto a substance, parts of the white light might be absorbed.
      Let's stipulate, for instance, that the substance absorbs yellow light.
      The rest gets reflected and observed by your eye.
    </p>

    <p>
      Your eye receives a combination of every color <em>other than yellow</em>.
      This amounts to the color directly opposite to it in a color wheel, which is blue in this case ( see <xref ref="fig-color-wheel-yellow-blue"/> ).
      This opposite color is called the <term>complementary color</term>.
      <idx><h>color wheel</h></idx> <idx><h>complementary color</h></idx>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-color-wheel-yellow-blue">
      <caption>Looking at the color wheel, when yellow is absorbed, a blue color is observed by your eye. The color wheel is taken from <c>8-leaf clover</c> on Wikimedia Commons.</caption>
      <image source="images/ch-04-laundry/sec-where-do-colors-come-from/color-wheel-yellow-blue.png" width="50%"/>
    </figure>

    <example>
      <title>Leaves and Chlorophyll</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Leaves are green because there is a pigment called <term>chlorophyll</term> in it.
          What is the approximate wavelength that chlorophyll absorbs?
          Reference <xref ref="fig-visible-spectrum"/> and <xref ref="fig-color-wheel-yellow-blue"/>.
          <idx><h>chlorophyll</h></idx> <idx><h>pigment</h></idx>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Looking at <xref ref="fig-color-wheel-yellow-blue"/>, chlorophyll absorbs violet light, which has a wavelength of about <quantity><mag>400</mag> <unit prefix="nano" base="meter" /></quantity>.
          Interestingly, this is not entirely accurate <mdash /> chlorophyll actually absorbs both red and blue light, which mix to violet.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
